   </property>
   <property name="text">
    <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here is the mixing stage in which (left to right) Koji, rice, and yeast are mixed in a container of water.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Koji is a mold (typically &lt;span style=&quot; font-style:italic;&quot;&gt;Aspergillus oryzae&lt;/span&gt;) grown on steamed rice by carefully incubating it in warm, humid conditions. The mold then breaks down the rice's starches into sugars, which yeast then ferments into alcohol.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sake rice, known as &lt;span style=&quot; font-style:italic;&quot;&gt;shuzo kotekimai&lt;/span&gt;, is a special variety polished to remove outer layers rich in proteins and fats, leaving behind the starchy core, which provides a clean, fermentable base essential for producing refined flavor and aroma in sake.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Yeast in sake brewing is a microorganism added after koji converts rice starches to sugars; it consumes these sugars and produces alcohol and aromatic compounds, playing a crucial role in determining the flavor, aroma, and alcohol content of the final drink.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;After mixing the ingredients, the mixture starts to ferment and rise.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
   </property>
